\newpage
\begin{verbatim}

%
% RULE RActive
< m1: I1::VERBPPROP{VERBPPROPrec1}
                            [mu1,
                             predrel/VERBP{VERBPrec1}
                                    [mu4,
                                     head/I2::VERB{VERBrec1}[mu3],
                                     mu2],
                             mu5
                            ]
>

< m : I3::VERBPPROP{VERBPPROPrec2}
                            [mu1,
                             predrel/VERBP{VERBPrec1}
                                    [mu4,
                                     head/I2::VERB{VERBrec1}[mu3],
                                     mu2],
                             mu5
                            ]
>


   MATCHCONDITIONS
      <
       (* m1:  EXIST (mu1,[subjrel/I11::NPVAR{NPVARrec1}])  ->
               NOT EXIST (mu2,[I21:rel2/NPVAR{NPVARrec1}])
          I11: NPVARrec1.nphead = theyNP
          I21: rel2 IN [objrel,indobjrel] *)
       I1: VERBPPROPrec1.voice = omegavoice
       I2: VERBrec1.modus = Omegamodus
       I3: VERBPPROPrec2.voice = active
      >

   PARAMETERS
      <
       voicepar : voicetype
      >
  
      COMP
         <
          C1: (voicepar = active AND 
               active IN VERBrec1.possvoices)
              OR
              (voicepar = passive AND 
               NOT passive IN VERBrec1.possvoices)
              OR
              (voicepar = passive AND
               EXIST (mu2, [objrel/EMPTYVAR{}]))          
          A1: VERBPPROPrec2 := VERBPPROPrec1;
              VERBPPROPrec2.voice :=  active
         >
      DECOMP
         <
          C1: active IN VERBrec1.possvoices
          A1: VERBPPROPrec1 := VERBPPROPrec2;
              VERBPPROPrec1.voice := omegavoice;
              voicepar := active 
         >


&

\end{verbatim}
\newpage
\begin{description}
\item[* name:] RActive
\item[* relevant examples:] She quickly turned on the radio
\item[* language:] (Dutch, English, Spanish) English
\item[* kind of rule:] (M-rule, S-rule, Transferrule) M-rule
\item[* subgrammar:] Verbppropformation
\item[* class:] (name of rule, transformation or filter class) RC: VoiceRules
\item[* date of creation:] 20-01-88
\item[* author:] margreet sanders
\item[* copied from:] dutch RActive
\item[* modification:]\mbox{}
\begin{enumerate}
\item date: ../../..\ \ \ \ \ author: 
\item date: ../../..\ \ \ \ \ author:
\end{enumerate}
\item[* task:] To generate Active voice structures
\item[\ \ strategy:] (also: deviations from accepted proposals) For mapping of 
actives on passives, see below under `parameters'.\\
Note that in this rule actives are formed even if there is an EMPTY subject. 
Depending on the formulation of the EMPTYSubjSubst rule, such a sentence may 
run dry in case it is infinite. An alternative is made in the next rule, where 
an active may translate into a passive in case there is an EMPTY subject. Thus, 
the tranlation of {\em Ik laat het boek (aan EMPTY) lezen } is {\em I let the 
book be read }, and perhaps also {\em I let one read the book }.
\item[\ \ rejected alternatives:]
\item[\ \ clarification of variables:]
\item[\ \ clarification of the splitting up into subrules:] dna
\item[\ \ clarification of the collapsing of rules:] (e.g. clarification of parameters)
The parameter voicepar is used to translate actives into passives and the
other way around exactly in those cases where this is necessary.
See also RPassive, where the same technique has been used.\\
See also [ODIJKJE.MRULES]GTRANSFER.trans and  [ODIJKJE\-.MRULES]\-ATRANSFER.trans
for the translation relations among the voice rules.\\
The use of the parameter works as follows: 
a Dutch passive construction is covered by the Dutch rule RPassive, 
with the parameter p:passive. This rule is mapped onto the English rules
RPassive(p:passive) and RActive(p:passive).
RActive(p:passive) is applicable only if the relevant verb cannot form a 
passive. As a result, e.g.\ {\em Er werd door Piet gedanst\/} will be subject to
the active rule in English because {\em dance} does not allow passivization.
A problem exists for sentences like {\em Er werd geprobeerd te dansen }, 
because {\em try } can be passived (but only with NP objects: the remedy was 
tried by all of us - $^*$to sing was tried by all 
of us) and will give the wrong translation {\em It was tried to dance } i.s.o.\ 
the correct {\em One tried to dance }. Perhaps in these cases the verb should 
be split up in the dictionary.\\
Dutch AanActive ({\em Ik {\em laat} het boek {\em aan} Jan zien/voelen/
horen etc.\/}) and 
DoorActive ({\em Ik {\em laat} het huis {\em door} Jan bouwen\/}) are 
always translated into English actives. The combination {\em laten zien (aan) } 
is NOT an ordinary AanActive, but an idiom, because its translation is the 
English verb {\em show }, which does not have an embedded clause at all.
For translation of Dooractives furthermore, if no active can be formed, a 
passive will be
formed. (There is always an active variant for AanActives.)\\
Currently, DoorActives are translated into Passives always as well, and the other 
way around, cf.\ e.g.\ {\em I had him killed\/} vs.\ {\em Ik liet hem doden\/}.

\item[\ \ differences analytical/generative version:] In analysis the voicepar
can be active only.
\item[\  \ suggested semantics:]
\item[\ \ presuppositions:]\mbox{}
\begin{enumerate}
\item dictionary:\\

\item rules:\\
It is assumed that varlists mu4 and mu5 are needed in the model to harbour ADVs that 
have been inserted there in the previous rule class, RC\_advvarrules. Other 
places for ADVs fall under the the existing varlists. It is assumed that no 
ADVVARs have been inserted as left hand sister of the subject (see next rule).
\end{enumerate}
\item[\ \ consequences in the grammar:]
\item[\ \ translation aspects:]\mbox{}
\begin{enumerate} 
\item within this language:\\

\item in other languages:\\
see above under `parameters'
\end{enumerate}
\item[* relevant literature/documents:]
\item[\ \ remarks:]\mbox{}
\begin{enumerate}
\item problems:\\

\item modifications:\\

\end{enumerate}
\end{description}

\newpage
\begin{verbatim}
% RULE RPassive1
< m1: I1::VERBPPROP{VERBPPROPrec1}
                   [I6:mu6,
                    I5:subjrel/T1,
                    I7:mu1,
                       predrel/VERBP{VERBPrec1}
                              [mu4,
                               head/I2::VERB{VERBrec1}[mu3],
                               I8:mu2
                              ],
                       mu5
                   ]
>

< m : I3::VERBPPROP{VERBPPROPrec2}
                   [I6:mu6,
                    I7:mu1,
                    predrel/VERBP{VERBPrec1}
                           [mu4,
                            head/I4::VERB{VERBrec3}[mu3],
                            I8:mu2,
                            byobjrel/VARPREPP{VARPREPPrec1}
                                    [head/PREP(KEY_doorbyprepkey){PREPrec1},
                                     I5:objrel/T1
                                    ]
                           ],
                    mu5
                   ]
>

   MATCHCONDITIONS
      <
       I1: VERBPPROPrec1.voice = omegavoice AND
           VERBPPROPrec1.thetavp IN [vp120, vp123] 
       I2: VERBrec1.modus = omegamodus
       I3: VERBPPROPrec2.voice = passive AND
           VERBPPROPrec2.thetavp IN [vp120, vp123]
       I4: VERBrec3.modus = participle
       I5: T1.CAT IN [ NPVAR, CNVAR, EMPTYVAR ]
       I6: ALL (mu6, leftdislocrel/..)
       I7: NOT EXIST (mu1, [leftdislocrel/..])
       I8: NOT EXIST (mu2, [objrel/EMPTYVAR{}])
      >

   PARAMETERS
      <
       voicepar : voicetype
      >

      COMP
         <
          C1: (voicepar = passive AND 
               passive IN VERBrec1.possvoices)
              OR
              (voicepar = active AND 
               NOT active IN VERBrec1.possvoices AND
               passive IN VERBrec1.possvoices) 
              OR
              (voicepar = active AND
               active IN VERBrec1.possvoices AND
               passive IN VERBrec1.possvoices AND
               T1.CAT IN [EMPTYVAR] )
              (* OR
                (voicepar = active AND
                 active IN VERBrec1.possvoices AND
                 passive IN VERBrec1.possvoices AND
                 (T1.CAT IN [NPVAR] WITH T1.REC.NPhead = theyNP)) *)
            C11: T1.CAT IN [ NPVAR ]
            A11: VARPREPPrec1.synppefs := [synNP];
                 VARPREPPrec1.mood := T1.REC.mood;
                 VARPREPPrec1.specQ := T1.REC.specQ 
            C12: T1.CAT IN [ CNVAR ]
            A12: VARPREPPrec1.synppefs := [synNP];
                 VARPREPPrec1.mood := relativexpmood;
                 VARPREPPrec1.specQ := yesspec
            C13: T1.CAT IN [ EMPTYVAR ]
            A13: VARPREPPrec1.synppefs := [synEMPTY];
                 VARPREPPrec1.mood := nowh;
                 VARPREPPrec1.specQ := yesspec
          A1: VERBrec3 := VERBrec1;
              VERBrec3.modus := participle;
              VERBPPROPrec2 := VERBPPROPrec1;
              VERBPPROPrec2.voice := passive;
              VARPREPPrec1 := { actsubcefs  : [doorby]
                                headkey     : KEY_doorbyprepkey 
                                thetapp     : pp120 
                                retro       : PREPrec1.retro
                                deixis      : PREPrec1.deixis
                                aspect      : PREPrec1.aspect
                                class       : PREPrec1.class }
            (* the other attributes keep default value *)
         >

      DECOMP
         <
          C1: passive IN VERBrec3.possvoices AND
              VARPREPPrec1 = PREPPrecdef EXCEPT FOR
                 [headkey, actsubcefs, synppefs, thetapp, mood,
                  retro, aspect, deixis, class, specQ] AND
              VARPREPPrec1 = { headkey     : KEY_doorbyprepkey
                               actsubcefs NI doorby
                               thetapp     : pp120 
                               retro       : PREPrec1.retro
                               deixis      : PREPrec1.deixis
                               aspect      : PREPrec1.aspect
                               class       : PREPrec1.class }
            C11: T1.CAT IN [ NPVAR ] WITH
                 ( VARPREPPrec1.mood = T1.REC.mood AND
                   VARPREPPrec1.specQ = T1.REC.specQ ) AND
                 synNP IN VARPREPPrec1.synppefs 
            A11: @
            C12: T1.CAT IN [ CNVAR ] AND
                 synNP IN VARPREPPrec1.synppefs AND
                 VARPREPPrec1.mood = relativexpmood AND
                 VARPREPPrec1.specQ = yesspec
            A12: @
            C13: T1.CAT IN [ EMPTYVAR ] AND
                 synEMPTY IN VARPREPPrec1.synppefs AND
                 VARPREPPrec1.mood = nowh AND
                 VARPREPPrec1.specQ = yesspec
            A13: @
          A1: VERBrec1 := VERBrec3;
              VERBrec1.modus := omegamodus;
              VERBPPROPrec1 := VERBPPROPrec2;
              VERBPPROPrec1.voice := omegavoice;
              voicepar := passive
         >

&

\end{verbatim}
\newpage
\begin{description}
\item[* name:] RPassive1
\item[* relevant examples:]\mbox{}\\
CA11: She quickly turn on the radio $\rightarrow$ 
quickly turned on the radio by she ($\rightarrow$ the radio was quickly turned 
on by her)\\
His colleagues tell he of other possibilities $\rightarrow$ told he
of other possibilities by his colleagues\\
CA12: (the sound) which disturb I $\rightarrow$ (the sound) disturbed I by 
which\\
CA13: [I had] EMPTY build a house in the garden $\rightarrow$ [I had] built a 
house by EMPTY in the garden\\ \mbox{}
[I let] EMPTY hear the music $\rightarrow$ [I let] heard the music by EMPTY
\item[* language:] (Dutch, English, Spanish) English
\item[* kind of rule:] (M-rule, S-rule, Transferrule) M-Rule
\item[* subgrammar:] Verbppropformation
\item[* class:] (name of rule, transformation or filter class) RC: VoiceRules
\item[* date of creation:] 18-01-88
\item[* author:] margreet sanders
\item[* copied from:] Dutch RPassive
\item[* modification:]\mbox{}
\begin{enumerate}
\item date: 9/11/89\ \ \ \ \ author: margreet sanders\\
Extended the rule to work also when the parameter is active and the verb can be 
active, but there is an EMPTY subject. This will occur only for embedded 
sentences ({\em men } and the generalized {\em je } and {\em ze } are now 
considered basic expressions, not EMPTYs, and the only other way to have an 
EMPTY subject is to make a passive, in which case the parameter is passive).
In case the translation {\em He let one hear the music } is considered bad 
English (see the subject EMPTYsubst rule: is {\em one } only allowed for finite 
sentences?), the alternative will now be: {\em He let the music be heard }.
\item date: ../../..\ \ \ \ \ author: 
\end{enumerate}
\item[* task:] To form structures in passive Voice, in those cases where no 
extra restrictions exist on T1.
\item[\ \ strategy:] (also: deviations from accepted proposals)
\item[\ \ rejected alternatives:]
\item[\ \ clarification of variables:]
\item[\ \ clarification of the splitting up into subrules:] dna
\item[\ \ clarification of the collapsing of rules:] (e.g. clarification 
of parameters) See RActive
\item[\ \ differences analytical/generative version:] \mbox{}
\begin{enumerate} 
  \item The voice parameter can only become passive in analysis, but may be 
active in generation
  \item In analysis, {\em doorby\/} must be {\em in\/} the actsubcefs, instead 
of the only element in the set.
\end{enumerate}
\item[\  \ suggested semantics:]
\item[\ \ presuppositions:]\mbox{}
\begin{enumerate}
\item dictionary:\\
The synpatterns of the prep {\em by\/} must contain at least [synNP, synQSENT]. 
(see next rule).
\item rules:\\
The shift rules must be sensitive to wh-NPVARs inside VARPREPPs.
\end{enumerate}
\item[\ \ consequences in the grammar:]
\item[\ \ translation aspects:]\mbox{}
\begin{enumerate} 
\item within this language:\\

\item in other languages:\\

\end{enumerate}
\item[* relevant literature/documents:]
\item[\ \ remarks:]\mbox{}
\begin{enumerate}
\item problems:\\
\item modifications:\\

\end{enumerate}
\end{description}

\newpage
\begin{verbatim}
% RULE RPassive2
< m1: I1::VERBPPROP{VERBPPROPrec1}
                   [I6:mu6,
                    I5:subjrel/SENTENCEVAR{SENTENCEVARrec1},
                    I7:mu1,
                    predrel/VERBP{VERBPrec1}
                           [mu4,
                            head/I2::VERB{VERBrec1}[mu3],
                            I8:mu2
                           ],
                    mu5
                   ]
>

< m : I3::VERBPPROP{VERBPPROPrec2}
                   [I6:mu6,
                    I7:mu1,
                    predrel/VERBP{VERBPrec1}
                           [mu4,
                            head/I4::VERB{VERBrec3}[mu3],
                            I8:mu2,
                            byobjrel/VARPREPP{VARPREPPrec1}
                                    [head/PREP(KEY_doorbyprepkey){PREPrec1},
                                     I5:objrel/SENTENCEVAR{SENTENCEVARrec1}
                                    ]
                           ],
                    mu5
                   ]
>

   MATCHCONDITIONS
      <
       I1: VERBPPROPrec1.voice = omegavoice AND
           VERBPPROPrec1.thetavp IN [vp120, vp123] 
       I2: VERBrec1.modus = omegamodus
       I3: VERBPPROPrec2.voice = passive AND
           VERBPPROPrec2.thetavp IN [vp120, vp123]
       I4: VERBrec3.modus = participle
       I5: SENTENCEVARrec1.mood IN 
                 [whinterrogative, yesnointerrogative]
       I6: ALL (mu6, leftdislocrel/..)
       I7: NOT EXIST (mu1, [leftdislocrel/..])
       I8: NOT EXIST (mu2, [objrel/EMPTYVAR{}])

      >

   PARAMETERS
      <
       voicepar : voicetype
      >

      COMP
         <
          C1: (voicepar = passive AND 
               passive IN VERBrec1.possvoices)
              OR
              (voicepar = active AND 
               NOT active IN VERBrec1.possvoices)
          A1: VERBrec3 := VERBrec1;
              VERBrec3.modus := participle;
              VERBPPROPrec2 := VERBPPROPrec1;
              VERBPPROPrec2.voice := passive;
              VARPREPPrec1 := { actsubcefs  : [doorby]
                                headkey     : KEY_doorbyprepkey
                                synppefs    : [synQSENT] 
                                thetapp     : pp120 }
            (* the other attributes keep default value *)
         >

      DECOMP
         <
          C1: passive IN VERBrec3.possvoices AND
              VARPREPPrec1 = PREPPrecdef EXCEPT FOR
                    [headkey, actsubcefs, synppefs, thetapp] AND
              VARPREPPrec1 = { headkey     : KEY_doorbyprepkey
                               actsubcefs NI doorby
                               synppefs   NI synQSENT
                               thetapp     : pp120 }
          A1: VERBrec1 := VERBrec3;
              VERBrec1.modus := omegamodus;
              VERBPPROPrec1 := VERBPPROPrec2;
              VERBPPROPrec1.voice := omegavoice;
              voicepar := passive
         >

&

\end{verbatim}
\newpage
\begin{description}
\item[* name:] RPassive2
\item[* relevant examples:]\mbox{}\\
{[What they want of me]} surprises me $\rightarrow$ surprised me by what they 
want of me (This is a wh-QSENT or an NP)\\
?[Whether or not I should go] puzzles me $\rightarrow$ puzzled me by whether or 
not I should go
\item[* language:] (Dutch, English, Spanish) English
\item[* kind of rule:] (M-rule, S-rule, Transferrule) M-Rule
\item[* subgrammar:] Verbppropformation
\item[* class:] (name of rule, transformation or filter class) RC: VoiceRules
\item[* date of creation:] 18-01-88
\item[* author:] margreet sanders
\item[* copied from:] Dutch RPassive
\item[* modification:]\mbox{}
\begin{enumerate}
\item date: ../../..\ \ \ \ \ author: 
\item date: ../../..\ \ \ \ \ author: 
\end{enumerate}
\item[* task:] To form structures in passive Voice, in those cases where 
the subject is a sentence (also see below under `translation aspects').
\item[\ \ strategy:] (also: deviations from accepted proposals)
\item[\ \ rejected alternatives:]
\item[\ \ clarification of variables:]
\item[\ \ clarification of the splitting up into subrules:] dna
\item[\ \ clarification of the collapsing of rules:] (e.g. clarification 
of parameters) See RActive
\item[\ \ differences analytical/generative version:]
\begin{enumerate}
  \item The voice parameter can only become passive in analysis, but may be 
active in generation
  \item In analysis, {\em doorby\/} must be {\em in\/} the actsubcefs, instead 
of the only element in the set.
\end{enumerate}
\item[\  \ suggested semantics:]
\item[\ \ presuppositions:]\mbox{}
\begin{enumerate}
\item dictionary:\\
The synpatterns of the prep {\em by\/} must contain at least [synNP, synQSENT]. 
(see previous rule).
\item rules:\\
It is assumed that closed gerunds cannot be input to this rule ($^{*}${\em I 
was surprised by him coming)}. See also below under `translation aspects'.
\end{enumerate}
\item[\ \ consequences in the grammar:]
\item[\ \ translation aspects:]\mbox{}
\begin{enumerate} 
\item within this language:\\

\item in other languages:\\
It is assumed that a THATSENT cannot be input to this rule (a sentence like 
{\em I was surprised that he came\/} is (according to Jan Odijk) not a passive 
with prep-deletion before 
{\em that\/}, but a verbalised adjective; cf.\ {\em I was surprised at his 
coming\/}. It is unclear whether Dutch does allow THATSENTs as input: {\em ?Ik 
werd gehinderd doordat hij laat kwam\/}. If so, there is a translation problem.
\end{enumerate}
\item[* relevant literature/documents:]
\item[\ \ remarks:]\mbox{}
\begin{enumerate}
\item problems:\\
\item modifications:\\

\end{enumerate}
\end{description}

